content.thinking:
Wait, the rejection marking logic has a redundant/incorrect condition:
```js
if (!candidateIds.has(article.id) || !verified.accepted.has(article.id)) {
```
`verified.accepted.has(article.id)` is already false because of the `continue` above. So the condition simplifies to always true for articles not accepted. That's fine but the `candidateIds` check is redundant. Let me simplify to just mark all usable-but-not-accepted articles as rejected. But careful: if `rewrite.status === 'partial'` and an item's candidate was dropped because of a missing id (model omitted it), that's a model failure — arguably not a content rejection. But caching it as rejected would permanently skip it. Hmm. The model omitting an item is more like an unusable response. But `usable=false` is a content decision. We can't distinguish omitted-id from usable=false in the current return (both result in no candidate). Actually in rewriteBatch, candidates only include items where matches.length===1 AND acceptRewrite passes. If the model returned usable=false, no candidate. If the model omitted the id, no candidate. If heuristic rejected, no candidate.

Caching all as 'rejected' could permanently skip an article that the heuristic wrongly rejected (false positive) — but heuristic false positives are possible (e.g., number guard). If we cache the rejection, it never gets retried under this policy. That's acceptable? The PROMPT says failed items keep originals; caching avoids budget waste. But a heuristic false positive would permanently lose a valid rewrite. Hmm. Trade-off.

To be safe, only cache rejections when the candidate reached verification and was rejected there (i.e., a content decision), OR when the model explicitly said usable=false. But we don't distinguish usable=false from omitted.

Let me refine rewriteBatch to report which items were explicitly `usable=false` vs missing/rejected. I could return a `decisions` map: id → 'usable'|'unusable'|'missing'|'heuristic_rejected'. Then cache 'rejected' only for 'unusable' and verification-rejected; not for 'missing' (model failure) or 'heuristic_rejected' (could be a guard false positive → keep retrying? but that wastes budget too...).

Actually, guards are deterministic; if the guard rejects a candidate, retrying the same article with the same model/prompt/context will likely produce the same rejection → wasteful. But a retry might produce a different headline (temperature 0.1, but not fully deterministic) that passes. Hmm.

Given the low-cost priority, caching rejections (including heuristic) is defensible. But the "never display a bad rewrite" priority is satisfied by keeping originals. The only downside is a missed future good rewrite. Given deterministic guards and cached policy fingerprint, and that source changes invalidate the cache, I think caching all source-grounded rejections is fine and matches "cache validation decisions".

But the risk: a transient heuristic false positive permanently blocks. Let me reduce risk by only caching when we actually fetched context and the rewrite call returned a valid structured response (`rewrite.status` in ok/partial/rejected, meaning parse succeeded and items processed). That's what I have. `invalid_response` excluded. Good.

Simplify the condition and remove redundancy. Let me also make sure we don't mark articles that weren't in this batch.

Let me rewrite that block cleanly:
```js
if (['ok', 'partial', 'rejected'].includes(rewrite.status) && ['ok', 'partial', 'rejected'].includes(verified.status)) {
  for (const { article } of usable) {
    if (verified.accepted.has(article.id)) continue;
    article.rewriteStatus = 'rejected';
    article.rewriteFingerprint = fingerprint;
    article.rewriteSource = sourceFingerprint(article);
  }
}
```

But wait: `verified.status` when there are no candidates is 'rejected' (our ternary default). And `rewrite.status` 'rejected'. So items get marked rejected. Good.

Also `rewrite.status` could be 'partial' with some candidates; verified 'partial'. Items not accepted marked rejected. Good.

Edge: If `verified.status === 'provider_unavailable'` we still might mark rejected before the providerFailed check (the marking block runs before the provider check). That would cache a rejection due to provider failure — wrong! Let me guard: only mark when verified.status is not provider_unavailable/invalid_response. My condition already requires verified.status in ['ok','partial','rejected'], so provider_unavailable excluded. Good. But the marking block is placed BEFORE the providerFailed check; since provider_unavailable isn't in the list, it won't mark. Good.

Also `rewrite.status === 'budget_guard'` breaks earlier. Good.

